From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: macbox <macbox@localhost>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:43:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/34] a flat grid counts in 32 bits when the
 dispatch fits

The flat variant's invocation index and its divisions by the grid were 64-bit for every
dispatch. Intel GPUs have no 64-bit integer divider, so each of the six 64-bit div/mod became
a long software sequence per invocation. The Simulator's masked blit (SimStreamProcessorObjects
blit_texture_to_texture_with_mask, 1206 x 2622 in 32 x 32 threadgroups, one per frame) spent
about 3.6 of its 4.9 ms there on Arrow Lake.

Now a dispatch-uniform test (nx*ny*nz*N <= 2^32, each product checked before the next can
wrap) selects a 32-bit arm; past it the previous 64-bit arm runs unchanged. Lab on ARL
(Mesa 26.2.4): the captured kernel 4.85 -> 1.13 ms, Apple's metallib through the runtime
p50 5.0-5.6 -> 1.6-2.0 ms with byte-identical outputs.

New lane: a ragged direct dispatch (the 1206 x 2622 screen, 3-D grids) runs on the flat
variant and each thread runs once at its own position, checked against a CPU expectation.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 9dbcffd80ced40c59040562a792145df7fa92cce)
